0..73 Comment "/// A lambda over a composite path, against the composite of two lambdas."
74..77 Comment "///"
78..138 Comment "/// `q ↦ Path.step (Path.step q g) h` and the composite of"
139..218 Comment "/// `q ↦ Path.step q g` and `r ↦ Path.step r h` are one function. Each body"
219..295 Comment "/// agrees with the other once the machine takes the bind at the endpoint of"
296..316 Comment "/// `Path.step q g`."
317..320 Comment "///"
321..399 Comment "/// The composite\'s body binds `Path.step q g`, a transport stuck on `g`, into"
400..478 Comment "/// `t ↦ Path.step t h`. That transport returns the value it moves, whatever"
479..556 Comment "/// its path. So the bind is its continuation at that endpoint, the transport"
557..632 Comment "/// along `h` that the lambda writes. Inside an action\'s motive, the kernel"
633..707 Comment "/// reads the two codes by its own rules, which run the composite\'s calls."
708..711 Comment "///"
712..784 Comment "/// - see: #3824 — a call of a returning variable held by the same law"
785..863 Comment "/// - see: #4011 — the checker refused the composite against its two lambdas"
864..944 Comment "/// - see: #4005 — the action over the composite, which the code walk hands on"
946..984 Comment "/// The composite of `F` and then `G`."
985..988 Keyword "def"
